      "id": "prop-analysis-by-practicalpurposeprop-analysis-by-practicalpurpose-nov-dec-2025-props",
      "title": "Prop Analysis by PracticalPurposeProp Analysis by PracticalPurpose - Nov/Dec 2025 Props",
      "author": "practicalpurpose",
      "date": "2025-11-30",
      "thumbColor": "#111214",
      "byline": "Decision Desk TSR's Legal Correspondent analyses what's on the ballot alongside the Class 2 Election",
      "bodyHtml": "<div>For Decision Desk TSR, I took a deep look at all the Props on this month's ballot and determined how they may affect the subreddit if they were to pass. This is my personal analysis.</div><div><br></div><div><b>A list of all the <a href=\"https://thespinroom.miraheze.org/wiki/Subreddit_propositions#Proposed_Props\">Proposed Props</a> can be found on the TSR wiki:&nbsp;</b></div><div><a href=\"https://thespinroom.miraheze.org/wiki/Subreddit_propositions#Proposed_Props\">https://thespinroom.miraheze.org/wiki/Subreddit_propositions#Proposed_Props</a></div><div><br></div><div>While effectively little will change, how each of the Props on this ballot are classified will depend on whether Prop 29 passes or fails, as it ratifies a new Constitution, the basic framework for the whole sub government. The new proposed Constitution is an \"editable\" one so any other Prop on this ballot that changes the Constitution would be classified as an Amendment and would directly change the wording of the Constitution to comply. Props that do not change the Constitution would apply as sub law. Prop 29 faces the strongest hurdles of this cycle. To be ratified, 60% of the voting public need to vote YEA and then 2/3rds of the Mods need to sign-on to it.</div><div><br></div><div><b>Prop 26:</b> This is one of two proposed modifications to the Mod election schedule. It should require 60% of the electorate voting in favor and 50% of the Mods to pass (per the <a href=\"https://thespinroom.miraheze.org/wiki/Subreddit_propositions#Prop_11_-_Kansas-Max_Reformation_Act\">Kansas-Max Reform Act</a> under existing subreddit law or per <a href=\"https://thespinroom.miraheze.org/wiki/First_TSR_Constitutional_Convention#Article_IV_-_Props_and_Amendments\">Article 4 </a>under the new Constitution)</div><div><br></div><div>Effectively Prop 26 reduces the current 14-month Mod term to an alternating 6-month/8-month term for each class. This is because two Mod seats, as opposed to one, would be up for election every two months, and since there are an odd number of electable seats (seven in total), two classes of Mods would only be paired every other cycle. For example, during one cycle, Class 7 Mod and Class 1 Mod would be on the same ballot, but in the next cycle, Class 6 Mod would be on the same ballot as the Class 7 Mod. Class 7 and 1 would then be paired again on the following cycle and so on.</div><div><br></div><div><b>Prop 27:</b> Aside from some administrative tasks for the election staff, this is the second Prop on the ballot to modify the election schedule. This Prop puts Mod elections every month for each Mod seat, effectively reducing the 14-month terms to 7-month terms. This schedule part at the very least seems to be incompatible with Prop 26 so if both pass, the Mods may need to clarify which vote was certified first.</div><div><br></div><div>Like with Prop 26, passage of Prop 27 should require 60% of the voting public and 50% of the Mods assenting.</div><div><br></div><div><b>Prop 28:</b> This Prop makes it easier to apply for a Voter ID and requires all Mod candidates to have a Voter ID, adding a new 30 karma requirement to run for office.</div><div><br></div><div>While borderline, this Prop should also be subject to the 60% threshold as set forth by the Kansas-Max Reform Act and would be an Amendment under the new Constitution as Article 1 states \"The sub Electorate may elect 7 <b>members of the sub...</b>\" rather than \"members of the Electorate\" which would make candidates subject to Voter ID laws.</div><div><br></div><div><b>Prop 30:</b> There has been a longstanding debate within the community, especially in the General Chat, about the posting of AI images and video. Some Mods have been more strict with enforcement of this rule than others and there has been some debate about Sub Rule #10 should be interpreted.</div><div><br></div><div>For reference, Sub Rule #10: \"The posting or commenting of images generated by AI is strictly prohibited, and any posts or comments that break this rule will be removed accordingly.\"</div><div><br></div><div>Prop 30 attempts to clarify that the rule is aimed at AI-generated deepfakes and other such images, not AI-generated elections maps and data visualization. The Prop still states that it is up to Mod discretion to determine what is and isn't allowed, as it already is anyway, so I suspect even if this Prop passes, the debate will continue.</div><div><br></div><div>Current law states that any Prop that \"alter[s]\" a Sub Rule or alters \"the practice of how rules are enforced\" is subject to the 60% threshold, and this holds true also for the new Constitution under Article 5: \"Any change to enforceable Sub Rules requires the full approval of a Prop but that Prop must achieve more 60% approval from the Electorate.\"</div><div><br></div><div><i>So if we are to follow established sub law and procedure, all the Props on today's ballot require 60% to pass the first step.</i></div>",
      "published": true
    }
  ]
